WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:27.640
<v Ira Glass>A couple of months ago, I happened to see, for the first time as an adult, the old Disney cartoon Snow White. It's kind of amazing, really. It's Walt Disney's first animated feature, made back in 1937, and when you watch it, it almost feels more like an opera than like a modern movie. Snow White and the prince and the seven dwarfs each enter the film in this very stagey way, each of them singing a song that declares who they are and what it is they want.
<v Ira Glass>几个月前，我成年后第一次偶然看了那部老迪斯尼动画片《白雪公主》。真的，太不可思议了。那是华特·迪士尼在1937年制作的第一部动画长片，当你观看它时，它给人的感觉更像是一部歌剧，而不是现代电影。白雪公主、王子和七个小矮人各自以这种极具舞台感的方式登场，每个人都唱着一首歌，宣告他们是谁，以及他们想要什么。

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:34.110
<v Snow White>[SINGING] I'm wishing-- I'm wishing-- for the one I love--
<v Snow White>[唱歌] 我许愿——我许愿——为了我所爱的人——

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:39.130
<v Ira Glass>Snow White, for instance, sings to her reflection in a wishing well, and her voice echoes back.
<v Ira Glass>例如，白雪公主对着许愿井里的倒影唱歌，她的声音回荡着。

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:54.100
<v Snow White>[SINGING] Today. I'm hoping-- I'm hoping-- and I'm dreaming of, the nice thing-- the nice thing-- you say-- you say.
<v Snow White>[唱歌] 今天。我希望——我希望——我梦想着，美好的事物——美好的事物——你说——你说。

00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:28.850
<v Ira Glass>And in fact, she meets the prince that very day. So I mentioned this to my sister who works for Disney-- she's a film executive there-- that I'd seen Snow White and how it felt like an old opera and how old-fashioned the opening songs were. And she goes no, no, no, no, no. It's not that it's like an opera. What you're hearing is just the "I Wish" song. And she tells me this is a standard thing, that this is in lots of movies. And the way it works is this. The very first song that the main character sings, they declare what it is that they want, and then that is what gets the story going. It's the "I Wish" song.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，她就在那天遇见了王子。所以我跟在迪斯尼工作的姐姐提到了这件事——她是那里的电影主管——说我看了《白雪公主》，觉得它很像一部老歌剧，开场的歌曲多么过时。她说：“不，不，不，不，不。它不是像歌剧。你听到的是‘我希望’之歌。”她告诉我这是一种标准，在很多电影里都有。它是这样运作的：主角唱的第一首歌，他们宣告他们想要什么，然后这就推动了故事的发展。这就是“我希望”之歌。

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.130
<v Ariel>[SINGING] I want more.
<v Ariel>[唱歌] 我想要更多。

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.020
<v Ira Glass>For instance, the Little Mermaid, Karen says, sings about her wish to be human.
<v Ira Glass>比如凯伦说，《小美人鱼》唱的是她想成为人类的愿望。

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:50.160
<v Ariel>[SINGING] I want to be where the people are. I wanna see, wanna see them dancin'. Walking around on those-- what do call 'em? Oh-- feet!
<v Ariel>[唱歌] 我想去人类所在的地方。我想看，想看他们跳舞。走在那上面——叫什么来着？哦——脚！

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.700
<v Ira Glass>Quasimodo, in the Disney version of The Hunchback of Notre Dame sings this from his tower as his first song.
<v Ira Glass>在迪斯尼版的《巴黎圣母院》中，卡西莫多在塔楼上唱了这首歌作为他的第一首歌。

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:28.370
<v Quasimodo>[SINGING] Safe behind these windows and these parapets of stone. Gazing at the people down below me. All my life I watch them as I hide up here alone. Hungry for the histories they show me. All my life I wonder how it feels to pass a day. Not above them, but part of them. And out there--
<v Quasimodo>[唱歌] 安全地躲在窗户和石栏杆后面。凝视着下面的人们。我一生都在躲在这里独自观察他们。渴望着他们展示的历史。我一生都在想，过上一天会是什么感觉。不是在他们之上，而是他们的一部分。在外面的世界——

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:38.210
<v Ira Glass>OK, hold up. I'm going to stop that. That is just too uplifting. Anyway, this phenomenon, the "I Wish" song, goes way beyond Disney films.
<v Ira Glass>好了，打住。我得停一下。这太振奋人心了。总之，这种“我希望”之歌的现象，远远超出了迪斯尼电影。

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:44.740
<v Dorothy>[SINGING] Somewhere over the rainbow, way up--
<v Dorothy>[唱歌] 彩虹之上，高高之上——

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:49.150
<v Ira Glass>This, of course, is the "I Wish" that Dorothy delivers at the beginning of The Wizard of Oz to go over the rainbow.
<v Ira Glass>这当然是多萝西在《绿野仙踪》开头为了越过彩虹而发出的“我希望”。

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:59.010
<v Dorothy>[SINGING] There's a land that I heard of once in a lullaby.
<v Dorothy>[唱歌] 有个地方我在摇篮曲里听说过。

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:23.460
<v Ira Glass>It turns out lots of old stage musicals begin with the "I Wish" song. Fiddler on the Roof has the daughter singing matchmaker, make me a match. Funny Girl has Barbara Streisand singing about becoming a star. My Fair Lady opens with Julie Andrews wishing for a room somewhere, far away from the cold night air. And Stephen Sondheim, ever the virtuous in this kind of thing, in "Into the Woods" has six different characters singing the various "I Wishes" in the opening.
<v Ira Glass>事实证明，许多古老的舞台音乐剧都以“我希望”之歌开头。《屋顶上的提琴手》里女儿唱着《媒人，为我做个媒》。《妙女郎》里芭芭拉·史翠珊唱着想要成为明星。 《窈窕淑女》开场是朱莉·安德鲁斯希望在一个房间里，远离寒冷的夜晚空气。斯蒂芬·桑德海姆，在这种事情上一直是个行家，在《魔法黑森林》的开场中，有六个不同的角色唱着各自的“我希望”。

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:23.940
<v Baker's Wife>[SINGING] I wish--
<v Baker's Wife>[唱歌] 我希望——

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.380
<v Cinderella>[SINGING] The King is giving a Festival.
<v Cinderella>[唱歌] 国王正在举办节日。

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:26.820
<v Baker>[SINGING] More than life.
<v Baker>[唱歌] 胜过生命。

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:27.300
<v Jack>[SINGING] I wish--
<v Jack>[唱歌] 我希望——

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.740
<v Cinderella>[SINGING] I wish to go to the Festival.
<v Cinderella>[唱歌] 我希望能去节日。

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:29.700
<v Baker>[SINGING] More than riches--
<v Baker>[唱歌] 胜过财富——

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:30.660
<v Cinderella>[SINGING] And the Ball--
<v Cinderella>[唱歌] 还有舞会——

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.420
<v Jack>[SINGING] I wish my cow would give us some milk.
<v Jack>[唱歌] 我希望我的牛能给我们一些牛奶。

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:33.270
<v Baker's Wife>[SINGING] More than anything--
<v Baker's Wife>[唱歌] 胜过一切——

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.930
<v Baker>[SINGING] I wish we had a child.
<v Baker>[唱歌] 我希望我们有一个孩子。

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.680
<v Jack>[SINGING] Please, pal, please, pal--
<v Jack>[唱歌] 请，伙计，请，伙计——

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.140
<v Cinderella>[SINGING] I wish to go to the Festival.
<v Cinderella>[唱歌] 我希望能去节日。

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:40.610
<v Jack>[SINGING] I wish you'd give us some milk or even cheese-- I wish.
<v Jack>[唱歌] 我希望你能给我们一些牛奶，甚至奶酪——我希望。

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:41.100
<v Baker>[SINGING] I wish we might have a child.
<v Baker>[唱歌] 我希望我们能有个孩子。

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.080
<v Cinderella>[SINGING] I wish.
<v Cinderella>[唱歌] 我希望。

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:58.840
<v Ira Glass>My sister Karen said that even the Britney Spears movie Crossroads begins with an "I Wish" song. Britney is dancing on her bed in her underwear, singing into a spoon, like it's a microphone, to a Madonna song on her stereo about a girl wants to be a star, which is what she comes by the end of the movie.
<v Ira Glass>我姐姐凯伦说，就连布兰妮·斯皮尔斯的电影《十字路口》也以“我希望”之歌开头。布兰妮穿着内衣在床上跳舞，拿着勺子当麦克风唱歌，那是她立体声音响里放的一首麦当娜的歌，唱的是一个女孩想成为明星，而这正是她在电影结尾所达到的目标。

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:09.880
<v Britney Spears>[SINGING] I've had to work much harder than this. For something I want don't try to resist me. Open your heart to me--
<v Britney Spears>[唱歌] 我必须比这更努力工作。为了我想要的东西，不要试图拒绝我。向我敞开心扉——

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:22.380
<v Ira Glass>When somebody points out to you how common this is, you start to see the "I Wish" song everywhere. And if you're host of your own national radio show, you start to think why haven't we done that? Why don't we ever start the show with a song--
<v Ira Glass>当有人向你指出这有多普遍时，你就会开始到处看到“我希望”之歌。如果你是自己全国广播节目的主持人，你就会开始想，为什么我们没这样做过？为什么我们从不用一首歌来开始节目——

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:23.690
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐播放]

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.740
<v Ira Glass>--where I express my hopes and my dreams for what the show will be today.
<v Ira Glass>——在那里我表达了我对今天节目的希望和梦想。

00:04:27.740 --> 00:05:33.060
<v Ira Glass>[SINGING] Oh, the woman in Act 1 is wistful, but funny in a quiet way. And the guy in Act 2 has a tough job to do, a mission that's gone on 20 days. I only wish their stories will be gripping and special, even though it's not the news and they're not stars. You remember what they said, you'll mention them at dinner, and when their on you will not leave your cars. Because it's radio. Stories on the radio can be funny or emotional, can fill you with longing. My wish is that today's show lives up to this song. And bring on the conflict. Make the people speak. Bring on that music we use every week. I wish for radio, decent stories on the radio.
<v Ira Glass>[唱歌] 哦，第一幕中的女人很怀旧，但有一种安静的幽默感。第二幕中的那个家伙有艰巨的工作要做，一个已经持续了20天的任务。我只希望他们的故事会引人入胜且特别，即使这不是新闻，他们也不是明星。你会记住他们说的话，你会在晚餐时提到他们，当他们在广播时，你不会离开你的车。因为这是广播。广播里的故事可以是幽默的或感性的，可以让你充满渴望。我的希望是今天的节目不负这首歌。让冲突开始吧。让人说话。带来我们每周使用的那种音乐。我希望有广播，广播里有像样的故事。

00:05:33.060 --> 00:06:02.510
<v Ira Glass>[SPEAKING] Yes, friends. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life , distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass. You know, those old movies and shows with the "I Wish" songs, they were stories of people trying to achieve some dream, trying to get to some promised land. And here this week at This American Life, we filled our show with stories like that. Stories of dreamers, stories of people who want to reach a place that they've only heard about. We have three stories in three acts. Starlee Kine, David Rakoff and Hillary Frank.
<v Ira Glass>[说话] 是的，朋友们。来自芝加哥WBEZ，这是《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。你知道，那些带有“我希望”之歌的老电影和节目，它们是关于人们努力实现某种梦想、努力到达某种应许之地的故事。本周在《美国生活》，我们的节目也充满了这样的故事。梦想家的故事，那些想要到达他们只听说过的地方的人的故事。我们有三个故事，分三个章节。斯塔莉·凯恩、大卫·拉科夫和希拉里·弗兰克。

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:18.970
<v Ira Glass>[SINGING] Oh, I wish that you'll stay here and la-cha-da-da. Please don't say goodbye or I'll see ya. You know you want to hear the final words we have from Torey Malatia.
<v Ira Glass>[唱歌] 哦，我希望你会留在这里，啦-恰-达-达。请不要说再见，否则我会见到你。你知道你想听到托里·马拉蒂亚的最后感言。

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:25.360
<v Ira Glass>[SPEAKING] Stuff's coming up, friends. So stay with us, eh?
<v Ira Glass>[说话] 节目马上开始，朋友们。敬请关注，嗯？

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:50.590
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Across the Street from Heaven. Well, decades of "I Wish" songs, sung by cartoon characters, spawned an entire empire of "I Wish." Disney films led to Disney theme parks. And at least for now, until the whole thing gets bought up by a big cable company, they're still there, emitting a whole fog of "I Wish" over small children. Starlee Kine tells this story, a true story, about a wish from her own childhood.
<v Ira Glass>第一章。天堂街对面。几十年来，“我希望”之歌，由卡通人物演唱，催生了一个完整的“我希望”帝国。迪斯尼电影导致了迪斯尼主题公园的出现。至少目前为止，在这一切被一家大型有线电视公司收购之前，它们仍然存在，在小孩身上散发着一阵“我希望”的迷雾。斯塔莉·凯恩讲述了这个故事，一个真实的故事，关于她童年的一个愿望。

00:06:50.590 --> 00:07:07.470
<v Starlee Kine>I guess my mom was what you'd call overprotective. When I would sleep over at a friend's house, the parents would inevitably receive a call around 3:00 in the morning from my mom, asking if they please wouldn't mind holding a mirror up to my mouth to make sure I was still breathing. After that, it was pretty hard to get invited back.
<v Starlee Kine>我想我妈妈是个所谓的保护过度的人。当我到朋友家过夜时，我妈妈难免会在凌晨3点左右给对方父母打电话，问他们是否介意拿面镜子放在我嘴边，确认我是否还在呼吸。在那之后，我就很难再被邀请去了。

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:45.740
<v Starlee Kine>I wasn't allowed to play soccer because I might get brain damage or die of internal bleeding. I wasn't allowed to ride a school bus because the driver might kidnap me. I wasn't allowed to walk my dog down our peaceful suburban street. "It's much too dangerous out there," my mom would say. I pleaded with her for months and she finally agreed, on one condition. I could walk the dog one time around the block provided I leave a trail of popcorn behind me so I didn't get lost. Later, outside, when the neighbor kids asked me what I was doing, I pretended not to hear them.
<v Starlee Kine>我不被允许踢足球，因为我可能会脑损伤或死于内出血。我不被允许坐校车，因为司机可能会绑架我。我不被允许在宁静的郊区街道上遛狗。“外面太危险了，”我妈妈会说。我求了她好几个月，她终于同意了，但有一个条件：我可以遛狗绕街区一圈，前提是我必须留下一串爆米花作为踪迹，这样我就不会迷路。后来，在外面，当邻居家的孩子问我在做什么时，我假装没听见。

00:07:45.740 --> 00:08:19.990
<v Starlee Kine>This pattern would repeat itself many times over the years. I'd ask to do something, my mom would refuse. I'd beg her to change her mind, and when she finally did, the compromise she came up with was so strange and complicated that I'd often wish she'd just kept saying no. Take what happened with Disneyland. A kid growing up in LA can get tired of Disneyland at an early age. 10-year-olds talk about it as though it was a phase they went through when they were young, like when they used to kiss the bear on the Snuggles box. Yeah, Disneyland's all right, I guess. I mean, if you're into that sort of thing.
<v Starlee Kine>多年来，这种模式重复了很多次。我会要求做某事，妈妈会拒绝。我会求她改变主意，当她终于答应时，她想出的折衷方案是如此奇怪和复杂，以至于我经常希望她坚持拒绝就好了。就拿迪斯尼乐园的事来说吧。在洛杉矶长大的孩子很早就对迪斯尼乐园感到厌倦。10岁的孩子谈论它时，好像那是他们年轻时经历的一个阶段，就像他们过去亲吻《舒适》包装盒上的小熊一样。是的，迪斯尼乐园还可以，我想。我的意思是，如果你喜欢那种东西的话。

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:53.860
<v Starlee Kine>My sister and I, however, were different. By the time I was nine and she was six, we'd still never been to Disneyland. My mom thought it was way too dangerous. She arrived at this conclusion during one of her daily trials through the morning paper, scanning for dangers that could befall her children. Somewhere in the back, she'd found an article about a faulty ride at a fair in a small town somewhere in the Midwest, and that was enough to seal our fate for years. No matter how much evidence we provided to the contrary, no matter how much we begged or whined or threatened to stop eating and drinking for 30 days, she still wouldn't budge.
<v Starlee Kine>然而，我和我妹妹不同。当我九岁、她六岁时，我们还从未去过迪斯尼乐园。我妈觉得太危险了。她在每天浏览晨报的过程中得出这个结论，扫描着可能降临到她孩子身上的危险。在报纸的某个角落，她发现了一篇关于中西部某个小镇游乐场设施故障的文章，这就注定了我们多年的命运。无论我们提供多少相反的证据，无论我们怎么乞求、抱怨或威胁说要连续30天不吃不喝，她就是不为所动。

00:08:53.860 --> 00:09:21.550
<v Starlee Kine>But just as we were about to give up for good, my mother had a change of heart. Perhaps the guilt had finally gotten to her. In any case, she decided to throw us a bone. She still wouldn't take us to Disneyland. She hadn't totally lost her mind after all. But she would let us do the next best thing. For two weeks, we could stay at the Disneyland Hotel.
<v Starlee Kine>但就在我们准备彻底放弃时，我妈妈改变了主意。也许是愧疚感终于击中了她。总之，她决定给我们一点甜头。她仍然不会带我们去迪斯尼乐园。毕竟她还没完全疯掉。但她会让我们做第二好的选择：连续两周，我们可以住在迪斯尼酒店。

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:36.770
<v Starlee Kine>Judging by the look on her face, you could tell my mom was pretty pleased by this proposition. She beamed at us expectantly, her arms outstretched. All we could manage to do was shrug our shoulders and nod.
<v Starlee Kine>从她脸上的表情可以看出，我妈妈对这个提议感到非常满意。她期待地看着我们，双臂张开。我们唯一能做的就是耸耸肩，点了点头。

00:09:36.770 --> 00:10:09.330
<v Starlee Kine>When the time came to leave, we packed up our little suitcases. We threw in our favorite toys and games. I carefully folded in a fresh unopened pack of Mad Libs I'd been saving for a special occasion. My mom made sandwiches for the ride and packed extra clothes for us in case it got too cold or too hot. She turned the air conditioning on in the car and waited until it was nice and freezing inside before letting us in. She didn't want us to get heat stroke. Then she pulled out of our driveway, and we were off.
<v Starlee Kine>出发的时间到了，我们收拾好我们的小行李箱。我们扔进了我们最喜欢的玩具和游戏。我小心地折进了一包我一直留着准备特殊场合用的未开封的《疯狂填字》游戏。妈妈为旅途做了三明治，还为我们多带了衣服，以防太冷或太热。她打开车里的空调，等到里面凉得舒服了才让我们进去。她不想让我们中暑。然后她把车开出车道，我们就出发了。

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:18.490
<v Starlee Kine>15 minutes later, we were pulling into the parking lot of the Disneyland Hotel. My mom told me to keep track of where we parked, and I made a mental note that we were in the Goofy section.
<v Starlee Kine>15分钟后，我们开进了迪斯尼酒店的停车场。我妈让我记住车停在哪儿，我心里记下我们停在“高飞”区。

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:46.740
<v Starlee Kine>The Disneyland parking lot was literally across the street. It was filled to capacity with lines of cars stretching as far as the eye could see. Behind them, I could make out the top of Space Mountain. That's how close we were. We headed to the hotel and into the lobby. The receptionist looked at us and smiled. She leaned down and asked me where we were visiting from. I pretended that I didn't hear her.
<v Starlee Kine>迪斯尼酒店的停车场就在马路对面。它停满了车，车队一眼望不到头。在它们后面，我能隐约看到太空山的顶端。我们离得就是这么近。我们走向酒店进入大堂。接待员看着我们笑了笑。她俯下身问我是从哪里来的。我假装没听见。

00:10:46.740 --> 00:11:05.260
<v Starlee Kine>For the next three years, we'd go to the Disneyland Hotel for two weeks twice a year without ever crossing the street over into Disneyland proper. Whenever we forgot something, like a toothbrush or a raincoat, we'd buy a new one instead of driving the 15 minutes home to pick it up. This made us feel more like we were on vacation.
<v Starlee Kine>接下来的三年里，我们每年两次去迪斯尼酒店住两周，却从没跨过马路走进迪斯尼乐园本部。每当我们忘记什么东西，比如牙刷或雨衣，我们就会买新的，而不是开车15分钟回家去拿。这让我们感觉更像是在度假。

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:29.210
<v Starlee Kine>Before I ever saw the hotel, I pictured a giant treehouse with branches stretching in every direction and little Do Not Disturb signs hanging from their tips. But in reality, the rooms weren't all that different from any other hotels, which wasn't a bad thing. They had everything a little kid loves about staying in a hotel. The comforters were scratchy, and the beds were springy and fun to jump on. And we could've spent days pressing the button on the ice machine.
<v Starlee Kine>在我还没见过这家酒店之前，我把它想象成一个巨大的树屋，枝桠向四面八方延伸，尖端挂着“请勿打扰”的牌子。但实际上，房间和其他酒店没什么不同，这并不是坏事。它们拥有一切小孩子喜欢住酒店的原因：被子很粗糙，床很有弹性，跳起来很有趣。我们可以花上一整天按冰块机的按钮。

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:54.730
<v Starlee Kine>The only problem was that we were constantly being reminded of what we didn't have. There was a picture of the Magic Kingdom on the ashtrays, on the complimentary notepads, on the soap, even worked into the paisley pattern of the curtains. When you ordered room service, the kids' menu also doubled as a Mickey Mouse mask. You just had to poke out the two slits for eyes and place the hooks over your ears. Then when you looked in the mirror, you could see Mickey's face looking back at you.
<v Starlee Kine>唯一的问题是，我们不断地被提醒我们没有得到的东西。烟灰缸、免费记事本、肥皂上都有魔法王国的图片，甚至连窗帘的佩斯利花纹里都融入了它。当你点客房送餐时，儿童菜单还可以变身成米老鼠面具。你只需要戳出两个眼孔，然后把钩子套在耳朵上。然后当你照镜子时，你会看到米奇的脸回望着你。

00:11:54.730 --> 00:12:27.670
<v Starlee Kine>This wouldn't have been half as bad if not for the fact that the real Mickey Mouse wouldn't have been caught dead in that hotel. He was much too famous. Instead we had to settle for the second- and third-tier characters, who walked around like they owned the joint. We soon had more autographs by J. Worthington Foulfellow-- the oily fox who lures Pinocchio away from school-- than we knew what to do with. My sister and I would ask him when Mickey was coming, but he'd just shrug his shoulders and do a little dance.
<v Starlee Kine>如果真正的米老鼠会出现在那家酒店，情况可能还没那么糟。他太出名了。相反，我们不得不接受那些二三流的角色，他们到处走，就像那是他们的地盘一样。很快，我们就拥有了太多“J.沃辛顿·福尔福”——那个引诱匹诺曹逃学的油滑狐狸的亲笔签名，多到不知道该怎么办。我和我妹妹会问他米奇什么时候来，但他只会耸耸肩，跳一小段舞。

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:57.690
<v Starlee Kine>We had a perfect view of Disneyland from our room. We'd watch people line up outside the entrance an hour before it even opened. At night, we could see the glow of the Electric Light Parade moving down Main Street. At first we were sure mom would cave. There was just no way she couldn't. She taken us, her two precious children, her whole reason for living, to the funnest place on Earth, only to stop 50 feet shy of the fun. It was like she was dangling the fun in front of us to see how long we could  go on grabbing at the air before finally collapsing of exhaustion.
<v Starlee Kine>从我们的房间可以看到迪斯尼乐园的完美景观。我们会看着人们在开园前一小时就在入口处排队。晚上，我们可以看到电动花车游行沿着主街缓缓移动。起初，我们确信妈妈会妥协。她没理由不妥协。她把我们，她两个珍贵的孩子，她生活的全部意义，带到了地球上最有趣的地方，却在离快乐不到50英尺的地方停下了。就像她把快乐悬在我们面前，看我们要多久才会因为疲惫而瘫倒之前，一直抓着空气。

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:35.660
<v Starlee Kine>There was a monorail that took the hotel guests directly to the park. It looked like something out of the Jetsons. In the beginning, my sister and I hung out in front of the monorail all the time. We'd bring our coloring books and Connect Four pieces and just camp out. We'd even eat our lunch there. We were like dogs waiting behind the door for our owner to finally open it and let us out. Every time we heard footsteps, we'd perk up our ears, hoping it was our mom coming to tell us it was time. We were ready, but she never came.
<v Starlee Kine>有一条单轨列车直接把酒店客人送到公园。它看起来像是《杰森一家》里的东西。起初，我和我妹妹一直待在单轨列车前。我们会带着涂色书和四子棋棋子在那里驻扎。我们甚至在那里吃午饭。我们就像等在门后的狗，等着主人终于打开门放我们出去。每次听到脚步声，我们就竖起耳朵，希望是妈妈来告诉我们时间到了。我们准备好了，但她从未来过。

00:13:35.660 --> 00:14:09.260
<v Starlee Kine>To pass the time, my sister and I became experts at the various kid activities the hotel had to offer. We could assemble the Donald Duck popsicle stick faces with our eyes closed. The hotel had a fake beach  next to the pool even though the real beach was only 10 minutes away. Every hour, a judge with a whistle around his neck would give an award for the best sand castle. The prize was five Disney dollars, valid for use only at Disneyland. My sister and I always won because we never had to get up and leave. The other kids would barely have managed their first tunnel, while we would already be adding on a second level to the carriage house in back.
<v Starlee Kine>为了打发时间，我和我妹妹成了酒店提供的各种儿童活动的专家。我们可以闭着眼组装唐老鸭冰棒棍脸。酒店在游泳池旁边有一个假沙滩，尽管真正的沙滩距离那里只有10分钟路程。每个小时，一名脖子上挂着哨子的裁判会为最好的沙堡颁奖。奖品是五张迪斯尼美元，只能在迪斯尼乐园使用。我和我妹妹总是赢，因为我们不需要起身离开。其他孩子刚搭好第一个隧道，我们就已经开始给后面的马车房加第二层了。

00:14:09.260 --> 00:14:46.500
<v Starlee Kine>We eventually got to know the different people who worked there. The guys who sang in the fake barbershop quartet, who didn't like each other at all. The mime who frankly seemed to be phoning it in. The man who sold churos that we bought for dinner. My favorite, however, was a caricature artist. We'd always wait until the last night of our trip to get our caricature done. We liked to save it for the end. The caricature artist would ask what we enjoyed doing most, and we'd tell him whatever we were into at the time. When I was nine, I had just gotten a new dog. The caricature artist drew my dog and me riding Space Mountain. He made my dog look like Pluto. His paws are sticking up in the air.
<v Starlee Kine>我们最终认识了在那里工作的不同的人。在假理发店四重唱里唱歌的人，他们一点也不喜欢对方。那个看上去是在敷衍了事的默剧演员。卖吉事果的男人，我们把吉事果当晚饭买。不过，我最喜欢的是一位漫画艺术家。我们总是等到旅行的最后一晚才去画我们的漫画。我们喜欢把它留到最后。漫画艺术家会问我们最喜欢做什么，我们会告诉他当时喜欢什么。我九岁时，刚得到了一只新狗。漫画艺术家画了我骑在太空山上，还画了我的狗。他把我的狗画得像布鲁托。它的爪子向上伸着。

00:14:46.500 --> 00:15:31.290
<v Starlee Kine>My mom still has these caricatures. They cover an entire wall of her house, picture after picture, the way other parents display photos of their kids. If you look at them in order, you can actually see us grow up before your very eyes, our oversized heads and little bodies acquiring different hairstyles and fashions over the years. There I am in fourth grade, learning how to swim. The caricaturist drew me jumping into a tiny bucket of water. In fifth grade, I discovered boys, and you can see me and Kirk Cameron riding a tandem bicycle. Sixth grade was my dark phase, where I didn't think I was interested in anything. The caricaturist just drew me standing in line for the Pirates of the Caribbean, rolling my eyes and looking surly.
<v Starlee Kine>我妈妈现在还留着这些漫画。它们挂满了她家的一整面墙，一张接一张，就像其他父母展示孩子照片那样。如果你按顺序看它们，你实际上可以看到我们在你眼前长大，我们硕大的脑袋和小身体随着年龄的增长换上了不同的发型和时尚。看，那是四年级的我，正在学游泳。漫画家画我跳进一个小水桶里。五年级时，我发现了男生，你可以看到我和柯克·卡梅隆骑着双人自行车。六年级是我阴暗的时期，我觉得自己对什么都不感兴趣。漫画家只是画我排队等着坐加勒比海盗，翻着白眼，看起来很阴沉。

00:15:31.290 --> 00:16:14.430
<v Starlee Kine>The hotel was literally brimming with children, a lot like how I imagined Never Never Land to be. Everywhere you turned, you spotted one, hiding behind a potted plant or laying under the continental breakfast buffet table. It was like their parents had surrendered all control of them. You'd get in an elevator, and there'd be at least a dozen kids, dressed however they saw fit. One wore a snorkel on his head even though he wasn't going to the pool. One wore a sneaker on his right foot and a cowboy boot on his left. Several of them wore capes made out of hotel pillowcases. Within seconds, some kid would push every one of the elevator buttons, and the other kids, deep in conversation with one another, would clap distractedly. If there were any adults in there with us, they'd roll their eyes, but not even all that much. They knew this place was for us.
<v Starlee Kine>酒店里挤满了孩子，就像我想象中的永无岛一样。无论你转向哪里，都会发现一个，躲在盆栽后面，或者躺在欧式自助早餐桌下。就像他们的父母已经放弃了对他们的所有控制。你走进电梯，里面至少有十几个孩子，穿着他们觉得合适的衣服。一个人头上戴着呼吸管，尽管他不去游泳池。一个人右脚穿着运动鞋，左脚穿着牛仔靴。他们中的几个人戴着酒店枕套做的斗篷。几秒钟内，某个孩子就会按下每一个电梯按钮，而其他正沉浸在彼此谈话中的孩子们会心不在焉地鼓掌。如果电梯里有大人，他们会翻个白眼，但不会多说什么。他们知道这个地方是给我们的。

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:49.760
<v Starlee Kine>My sister and I made friends with these kids whenever we could. Timing was key. It was imperative that we catch them before they made the trek across the street. After that, we'd lose our edge. So every night we'd hang out by the checkout desk and await the new arrivals. We'd sidle up to them while their parents were checking in and ask them where they were from. We told them that we knew the hotel pretty well, so they should stick with us. We knew how to get stuff. "Would you like extra marshmallows with your hot chocolate?" we'd say. "The room service guy is a friend of ours. How about extra whipped cream?"
<v Starlee Kine>我和我妹妹尽可能地和这些孩子交朋友。时机很关键。必须在他们动身跨过马路之前抓住他们。在那之后，我们就失去了优势。所以每天晚上，我们都会在结账台旁闲逛，等待新来的客人。当他们的父母办理入住手续时，我们会溜到他们身边，问他们是从哪里来的。我们告诉他们我们很熟悉这家酒店，所以他们应该跟着我们。我们知道怎么搞到东西。“你们想在热巧克力里多加点棉花糖吗？”我们会说。“客房服务员是我们的朋友。多加点鲜奶油怎么样？”

00:16:49.760 --> 00:17:26.410
<v Starlee Kine>We'd spend a few hours with them or, if we were lucky and it rained, a full day. Then these kids would head off to the park. My sister and I would be waiting outside the monorail when they returned. They'd climb off, all loaded down with souvenirs, looking pink and exhausted. But in their eyes, we detected a change. They'd seen things we couldn't even begin to imagine, and we couldn't think of anything to say to them. After a few days when they checked out of the hotel, none of them bothered to say goodbye.
<v Starlee Kine>我们会和他们呆几个小时，或者如果运气好下雨的话，就呆上一整天。然后这些孩子会出发去乐园。当他们回来时，我和我妹妹会在单轨列车站台外等着。他们走下车，带着满满的纪念品，脸红红的，筋疲力尽。但在他们的眼里，我们察觉到了一种变化。他们见到了我们根本无法想象的事情，而我们想不出什么可说的。几天后，当他们从酒店结账退房时，没人会说再见。

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:54.860
<v Starlee Kine>We did finally make it to Disneyland as kids when I was 11 and my sister was 8, and it happened in the most random way possible, like a piano falling out of the sky onto our heads. One morning, long after my sister and I had stopped asking, my mom just said, "Hey, guys. Do you want to go to Disneyland?" It was a school day. We had no idea where this uncharacteristic burst of spontaneity was coming from, but my sister and I had learned by now not to question these things.
<v Starlee Kine>我和我妹妹最终在11岁和8岁时以孩子的身份去了迪斯尼乐园，这件事发生得非常随机，就像一架钢琴从天而降落在我们头上。一天早上，很久没再问过这事的我们，妈妈突然说：“嘿，伙计们。想去迪斯尼乐园吗？”那是个上学的日子。我们不知道这种突如其来的自发性是从哪来的，但我和我妹妹已经学会了不去质疑这些事情。

00:17:54.860 --> 00:18:32.350
<v Starlee Kine>Once we are finally inside the gates, my sister and I knew exactly where to go. There had been a map of the park with illustrations of all the different rides in the hotel lobby. We studied it so long we had it memorized. But the real Disneyland was different than the one we'd imagined for so long. It was strange to see that it was a natural place with long lines and smelly bathrooms. When I climbed onto a ride, I was surprised by how solid the seat felt. When you think of fantasy places in your head, you never think about the fact that they're plastic, that the overhead bar might pinch your neck. We thought it was great.
<v Starlee Kine>一旦我们最终进了大门，我和我妹妹就知道该去哪儿。酒店大堂里有一张公园地图，上面有所有不同游乐设施的插图。我们研究了太久，早就背下来了。但真正的迪斯尼乐园和我们想象了那么久的那个不同。奇怪的是，它是一个自然的地方，有长长的队伍和难闻的厕所。当我爬上一个游乐设施时，我对自己感觉到的座位那么坚硬感到惊讶。当你脑子里想到梦幻的地方时，你从没想过它们是塑料做的，头顶的栏杆可能会夹住你的脖子。我们觉得这很棒。

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:54.520
<v Starlee Kine>My mom, of course, wouldn't let us go on everything. She said the Matterhorn looked like it was about to fall apart, and we knew Thunder Mountain was out of the question. But we didn't care. She seemed more relaxed that day than we'd ever seen her. She laughed her head off in the tea cups. And when my sister got a paper cut from the salted pretzel wrapper, my mom just gave it a kiss instead of rushing her to the Emergency Room.
<v Starlee Kine>当然，我妈妈不会让我们玩所有的项目。她说马特洪峰看起来快要散架了，我们知道雷山当然也不可能。但我们不在乎。那天她看起来比我们见过的任何时候都放松。她在茶杯游戏里笑得前仰后合。当我妹妹被咸椒盐卷饼包装纸割破手指时，我妈妈只是亲吻了一下，而不是急着把她送进急诊室。

00:18:54.520 --> 00:19:21.090
<v Starlee Kine>We were getting our Mickey Mouse hats embroidered with our names when our mom came up to us and told us to follow her. She led us to the ticket office in front and asked the lady for three annual passes: one adult and two kids. The passes meant we could come to Disneyland any time we wanted that year for free. The lady took our ID photograph for the card, my sister and I each wearing the same stunned expression on our face.
<v Starlee Kine>当我们正要把米老鼠帽子上绣上我们的名字时，妈妈走到我们身边，叫我们跟着她。她带我们到前面的售票处，向女士要了三张年票：一张成人的，两张儿童的。这意味着我们那一年可以随时免费来迪斯尼乐园。那位女士为卡片拍了我们的身份证照片，我和我妹妹脸上都带着同样的震惊表情。

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:47.060
<v Starlee Kine>The thing is, we never went back that year. We didn't use the passes once. Before going, my sister and I had been sure that once we were there, we'd never want to leave. Nothing was more important than being allowed inside. But now that we'd actually seen what it was, it's not that we were disappointed, but it just didn't loom so big anymore. We kept meaning to go back, but other things kept coming up.
<v Starlee Kine>问题是，那一年我们再也没去过。我们一次都没用过这些票。在去之前，我和我妹妹很确定，一旦到了那里，我们就再也不想离开。没有什么比被允许进入更重要的了。但现在我们真的看到了它是什么，并不是说我们失望了，而是它不再显得那么重要了。我们一直打算回去，但总有别的事情发生。

00:19:47.060 --> 00:20:19.920
<v Starlee Kine>Finally, when I was 18, I returned. That year, my prom was held at the Disneyland Hotel. Being there as a teenager with my friends, it was hard to believe it had all ever happened. I didn't want anyone to find out, but I just knew the place too well. At one point, one girl wondered aloud where the bathroom was. "Oh, it's just down the hall about 20 paces," I said. "Past the storage room, but before the Lost and Found. Just make a left, then a right, and then another right, and you're there." Everyone stared at me, and I felt my cheeks go red.
<v Starlee Kine>最后，当我18岁时，我回来了。那一年，我的毕业舞会在迪斯尼酒店举行。作为一个十几岁的孩子和朋友们在那里，很难相信这一切真的发生过。我不想让任何人发现，但我就是太了解这个地方了。有一次，一个女孩大声问厕所在哪儿。“哦，顺着走廊走20步就到了，”我说。“过了储藏室，但在失物招领处之前。左转，再右转，然后再右转，就到了。”大家都盯着我，我感到脸颊发烫。

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:43.290
<v Starlee Kine>My mom, of course, initially didn't want me to go to prom at all. She worried about some kind of accident on the road, about the limo getting a flat and a crazy person picking us up and killing us all. About drinking, about my friends, about my dress, about my date. The only way I was able to calm her down was when I told her where the prom was being held-- the Disneyland Hotel. Her shoulders immediately untensed when I said the words.
<v Starlee Kine>当然，我妈妈起初根本不想让我去参加舞会。她担心路上发生意外，担心豪华轿车爆胎，担心疯子把我们接走杀掉。担心喝酒，担心我的朋友，担心我的礼服，担心我的舞伴。我能让她冷静下来的唯一方法就是告诉她舞会在哪里举行——迪斯尼酒店。我说出这两个词时，她的肩膀立刻放松了下来。

00:20:43.290 --> 00:21:22.950
<v Starlee Kine>In reality, of course, sending a group of teenagers off to be by themselves in a hotel is exactly the sort of thing a parent probably should worry about. And in retrospect, letting loose your daughters at six and nine years old without supervision of any kind in a hotel full of strangers and adults in masks was probably a lot more dangerous than walking across the street to take them on It's a Small, Small World. But my mom never saw it that way. For her, the Disneyland Hotel was always the safest place on Earth.
<v Starlee Kine>现实中，当然，让一群青少年独自待在酒店里确实是父母可能应该担心的事情。回想起来，让六岁和九岁的女儿在没有监督的情况下，在充满陌生人和戴面具成年人的酒店里散养，可能比过马路带她们去坐“小小世界”要危险得多。但我妈妈从不这么看。对她来说，迪斯尼酒店永远是地球上最安全的地方。

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:38.300
<v Ira Glass>Starlee Kine. Coming up, an experiment in trying to reach enlightenment involving a man and six to eight ounces of vegetable broth. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>斯塔莉·凯恩。接下来，一个尝试达到启蒙境界的实验，涉及一个男人和六到八盎司的蔬菜汤。节目将在一分钟后从芝加哥公共广播和公共广播国际公司继续播出。

00:21:38.300 --> 00:22:40.410
