00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:52.400
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose some theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, Promised Land. Stories of people who wish to get somewhere they've never been.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，并为你带来各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目主题：应许之地。关于那些希望到达他们从未去过的地方的人们的故事。

00:22:52.400 --> 00:23:07.810
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Two of our program. Act Two. Life in the Fast Lane. There's an ancient idea that fasting can lead to moments of enlightenment. Jesus fasted for 40 days in the desert. The Buddha fasted, as did Pythagoras and Gandhi and George Bernard Shaw.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第二章。第二章：快车道上的生活。有一种古老的观点认为禁食可以带来启蒙时刻。耶稣在沙漠中禁食了40天。佛陀禁食过，毕达哥拉斯、甘地和萧伯纳也一样。

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:31.860
<v Ira Glass>Is it possible that all that stands between us and genius and insight is food? Well, we wanted to find out if anyone could reach this promised land, and our contributor David Rakoff agreed to be the guinea pig in a little experiment. He went on a 20-day fast that was 14 days with no food, three days transitioning in and out on either side to find out if he would see God-- or some variant.
<v Ira Glass>难道阻碍我们获得天赋和洞察力的仅仅是食物吗？嗯，我们想弄清楚是否有人能到达这个应许之地，我们的撰稿人大卫·拉科夫同意成为这项小实验的豚鼠。他进行了一次为期20天的禁食，其中14天不吃东西，两边各过渡三天，看看他是否能见到上帝——或者某种变体。

00:23:31.860 --> 00:24:27.240
<v David Rakoff>Let's start with a showstopper, shall we? For the next three weeks, my path to enlightenment goes like this. I'm to wake up, head to the bathroom, and give myself an enema. You heard me. Warm water, Chamomile tea, and eight drops of lemon juice. I'm to hold it in for 15 minutes, lying on a towel on the bathroom floor, passing the time by reading some sort of spiritual literature. I opt for The New York Times. This is followed by a shower and a breakfast of 8 to 12 ounces of clear vegetable broth. At other points during the day, and I cannot say exactly when because this is copyrighted proprietary information, I will consume fruit juice, vegetable juice, herbal tea, and finally, more broth just before bed.
<v David Rakoff>让我们从一个压轴戏开始，好吗？在接下来的三周里，我通往启蒙的道路是这样的：我醒来，去洗手间，给自己灌肠。你没听错。温水、甘菊茶和八滴柠檬汁。我要把它憋住15分钟，躺在浴室地板上的毛巾上，通过阅读某种灵性文献来打发时间。我选择了《纽约时报》。接着是洗澡，早餐喝8到12盎司清澈的蔬菜汤。在一天中的其他时间，由于这是受版权保护的专有信息，我不能具体说是什么时候，我会喝果汁、蔬菜汁、草药茶，最后在睡前再喝一些肉汤。

00:24:27.240 --> 00:25:05.370
<v David Rakoff>I'm on an extended fast. I find my particular fast on the web. It's like a correspondence spa. For about $300, I am provided with an exhaustive regimen that includes the recipe for the special broth I will be drinking, along with a schedule for the additional juices and herbal teas that I will also be drinking. And I will be guided by the center's founder and president, a man I'll call Brian, based in California. I can email him with all of my questions. I make sure to tell my doctor, and Ira also agrees to check in with me every now and then.
<v David Rakoff>我正在进行一次延长的禁食。我在网上找到了我这种特殊的禁食法。这就像一个远程水疗中心。花大约300美元，我得到了一份详尽的方案，包括我将要喝的特殊肉汤的食谱，以及我将要喝的额外果汁和草药茶的时间表。我将由中心创始人和总裁指导，一个我称之为布莱恩的男人，他住在加利福尼亚。我可以通过电子邮件向他提出所有问题。我确保告诉了我的医生，艾拉也同意偶尔查看一下我的情况。

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:08.470
<v Ira Glass>David, this is the first day of your fast.
<v Ira Glass>大卫，这是你禁食的第一天。

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:09.380
<v David Rakoff>Yes.
<v David Rakoff>是的。

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:11.870
<v Ira Glass>Are you very, very hungry right now?
<v Ira Glass>你现在非常非常饿吗？

00:25:11.870 --> 00:25:21.170
<v David Rakoff>I'm not very, very hungry. I'm quite sleepy. And I'm finding it rather difficult to talk this much.
<v David Rakoff>我不算非常非常饿。我相当困。而且我发现要说这么多话相当困难。

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:23.380
<v Ira Glass>Wow! It's taxing.
<v Ira Glass>哇！这很费力。

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:28.700
<v David Rakoff>Yeah. And that kind of scares me, actually.
<v David Rakoff>是的。事实上，这让我有点害怕。

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:40.650
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>People who stop taking in carbohydrates have to make this switch from one kind of fuel to the other kind of fuel from carbohydrates to these very tiny fat cells known as ketones.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>停止摄入碳水化合物的人必须从一种燃料切换到另一种燃料，即从碳水化合物切换到这些被称为酮的微小脂肪细胞。

00:25:40.650 --> 00:26:01.970
<v David Rakoff>This is Dr. Lisa Sanders who, as part of a team at Yale, spent the past five years analyzing the science behind over 700 different diets. She's also written a book based on this work called The Perfect Fit Diet. On day one of the fast, she tells me my sluggishness is due to this switch from carbohydrates to ketones.
<v David Rakoff>这是丽莎·桑德斯医生，作为耶鲁大学团队的一员，她花了过去五年时间分析了700多种不同饮食背后的科学。她还根据这项工作写了一本书，名叫《完美的饮食》。在禁食的第一天，她告诉我，我的迟钝是由于这种从碳水化合物到酮的转换。

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:33.100
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>When that happens, people feel sort of tired and out of sorts. Some people describe feeling a headache. After a couple of days, people often return to feeling very normal even though they're not taking in any carbohydrates, even though they're not taking in their usual supply of gasoline. Then people say that they don't feel hungry at all. There's something about ketones that kills your appetite. Food is not an issue for them anymore.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>当这种情况发生时，人们会感到有点累和不对劲。有些人描述说会头痛。几天后，人们通常会恢复到感觉非常正常的状态，即使他们没有摄入任何碳水化合物，即使他们没有摄入平时的汽油供应。然后人们说他们根本不觉得饿。酮类物质有一种能抑制食欲的东西。对他们来说，食物不再是一个问题。

00:26:33.100 --> 00:26:56.930
<v David Rakoff>What I've been reading is that, yes, they do predict a few days at the beginning where it's going to be quite difficult, and then comes a kind of a clarity, peace, and clearheadedness. And I was wondering whether there was a physiological reason that you could think of that might account for this sort of enhanced clarity?
<v David Rakoff>我读到的是，是的，他们确实预测开始的几天会相当困难，然后会出现一种清晰、平静和头脑清醒的状态。我想知道是否有一种生理原因你可以想到可以解释这种增强的清晰感？

00:26:56.930 --> 00:27:13.060
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>Hm. Well, hm. Let me think about that for a second. Ketones are your brain's preferred fuel. So maybe there's something about that that makes people feel good.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>嗯。好吧，嗯。让我考虑一下。酮是你大脑的首选燃料。所以也许这其中有什么东西让人们感觉良好。

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:56.000
<v David Rakoff>The website where I bought this fast explains my changes in mood very differently. It is not about ketones or switching fuel sources. It is about toxins. According to my fasting center, as an urban North American living in the 21st century, I have a surpassingly toxic body with an average of 5 to 10 pounds of damaging impurities housed in my cells. My nerves and organs are apparently coated with this ubiquitous, sludgy film of judgment-clouding, character-distorting poison. Chemical fertilizers, remnants of medication, heavy metals, artificial colorings, all conspire to make me less than the best David I can be.
<v David Rakoff>我购买这个禁食方案的网站对我的情绪变化解释得完全不同。这与酮或切换燃料来源无关。这是关于毒素的问题。根据我的禁食中心，作为一个生活在21世纪的城市北美居民，我的身体极其有毒，我的细胞里含有平均5到10磅的有害杂质。我的神经和器官显然被覆盖着一层无处不在的、污浊的判断力云遮蔽、性格扭曲的毒素电影中。化学肥料、药物残留、重金属、人造色素，所有这些都勾结起来，让我无法成为最好的大卫。

00:27:56.000 --> 00:28:46.500
<v David Rakoff>Under this logic, the reason I feel so rotten to these first few days of my fast, is that as the poisons leave the relatively inert resting places they have carved out in my cells and pass through my skin and, most importantly, my colon, they will once again exert their poisonous effects. Stuff that hurts going in will hurt coming out, I am told. And it's all about stuff coming out, so to speak. Every fast I research is intensely concerned with notions of purity and cleanliness and the need to flush oneself out, whether through laxative teas and chugging a gallon of saltwater or, in my case, the enemas. Did I mention that I have to do a daily enema?
<v David Rakoff>根据这个逻辑，我在这禁食头几天感到如此糟糕的原因是，当毒素离开它们在细胞中刻下的相对惰性的静止位置，并通过我的皮肤，最重要的是，我的结肠排出时，它们会再次发挥它们的毒性作用。我被告知，进去时伤害身体的东西，出来时也会伤害身体。而且一切都是关于“出来”的东西，可以这么说。我研究的每一次禁食都非常关注纯洁和清洁的概念，以及需要通过泻药茶和灌下一加仑盐水，或者在我的情况下，灌肠来冲洗自己的需求。我提到过我必须每天灌肠吗？

00:28:46.500 --> 00:29:08.620
<v David Rakoff>The fixation is on the eradication of these supposed pollutants, 5 to 10 pounds of them. That is the size of a robust newborn baby. It seems like an awful lot of poison in an ostensibly healthy person. Dr. Sanders says there's little scientific proof of this.
<v David Rakoff>这种执念在于根除这些所谓的污染物，5到10磅之多。那是一个健壮的新生儿的大小。对于一个表面上健康的人来说，这似乎是太多的毒素了。桑德斯医生说，这几乎没有什么科学证明。

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:25.260
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>I've never seen any evidence of 5 to 10 pounds of toxins stored in our bodies. Most things don't do much and don't get around that much. Our cells are pretty selective about what they take up and what they don't take up as far as I know, as far as we've been able to show.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>我从未见过任何证据表明我们的身体里储存了5到10磅的毒素。大多数东西不会有太大作用，也不会到处跑。据我所知，据我们能够证明的程度，我们的细胞对摄取什么和不摄取什么是非常有选择性的。

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:27.180
<v David Rakoff>Uh-huh.
<v David Rakoff>嗯哼。

00:29:27.180 --> 00:29:37.650
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>And purification of your body, we live in a dirtier and dirtier world. And yet our lives are longer and longer. Go figure.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>关于你的身体净化，我们生活在一个越来越脏的世界里。然而我们的寿命却越来越长。谁知道呢。

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:41.980
<v David Rakoff>So would this be pseudoscience?
<v David Rakoff>那么这会是伪科学吗？

00:29:41.980 --> 00:30:44.200
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>I think so. I mean, there's a lot of stuff we don't know about the human body. There's no question about it. And so that's why I can't just out of hand say this is a whole bunch of hogwash. We should have studies. If this is true, then they should be able to show us that people who do this are healthier in some measurable, tangible way than people who don't do it and not just those who volunteer to do it. Because obviously, this is going to appeal to somebody who's very interested in their health and their well-being, somebody who cares about what they eat and that they exercise correctly. So this is not going to be science. This might be beliefs. And societies are filled with beliefs about things that are good for you, that may or may not be-- that may have no scientific merit whatsoever, but that doesn't mean that they don't work. It doesn't mean that they do work either.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>我想是的。我的意思是，关于人体还有很多我们不知道的事情。毫无疑问。所以这就是为什么我不能直接说这是一堆胡说八道。我们应该进行研究。如果这是真的，那么他们应该能够向我们展示，做这种事情的人在某些可衡量的、有形的方式上比不做的人更健康，而不仅仅是那些自愿这样做的人。因为显然，这会吸引那些非常关注自己健康和幸福的人，那些关心自己吃什么、锻炼是否正确的人。所以这不是科学。这可能是信仰。社会充满了关于什么是对你有好处的信仰，这些信仰可能——可能没有任何科学价值，但这并不意味着它们不起作用。这也不意味着它们确实起作用。

00:30:44.200 --> 00:31:00.960
<v David Rakoff>The fasting program I'm on doesn't just promise weight loss, greater energy, and enhance virility, but also insight, clarity, and inner peace. And to be quite frank, I could do with some inner peace.
<v David Rakoff>我参加的禁食计划不仅承诺减肥、增强活力和增强性能力，还承诺洞察力、清晰和内心的平静。说实话，我确实需要一些内心的平静。

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:04.840
<v David Rakoff>Do you have any advice for me?
<v David Rakoff>你对我有什么建议吗？

00:31:04.840 --> 00:31:17.790
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>Well, I would not think of this as a health experiment. I wouldn't think of this is a weight loss experiment. I would think of this as a spiritual experiment.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>嗯，我不会把这当作一个健康实验。我不会把它当作一个减肥实验。我会把它当作一个精神实验。

00:31:17.790 --> 00:31:18.930
<v David Rakoff>Uh-huh.
<v David Rakoff>嗯哼。

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:55.210
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>I have to say, I read about these holy people who fast for weeks at a time and have these visions. And you really don't know what to do with it because you know that something happened to them, but they interpreted what happened to them through their spiritual interests. I'd be interested in seeing how a rationalist would interpret these same feelings. I hope you see something. I hope you have a good experience.
<v Dr. Lisa Sanders>我必须说，我读过关于这些禁食数周并产生幻觉的圣人。你真的不知道该怎么处理它，因为你知道他们身上发生了什么，但他们通过他们的精神兴趣解释了发生在他们身上的事情。我很想看看一个理性主义者会如何解释这些同样的感受。我希望你看到一些东西。我希望你有一个好的体验。

00:31:55.210 --> 00:32:22.710
<v David Rakoff>By day four, the day I'm supposed to have turned the corner, I am certainly feeling better. I am no longer hungry, and the headaches and listlessness are largely gone. I feel more or less normal. But that's it. No brilliant shafts of light piercing the clouds, no strange hallucinatory visitations. By day five, I have more energy. I even manage to make it to the gym.
<v David Rakoff>到了第四天，我应该转弯的那一天，我确实感觉好多了。我不再饿了，头痛和无精打采也基本消失了。我感觉或多或少正常。但就是这样。没有明亮的光束刺破云层，没有奇怪的幻觉访问。到了第五天，我有了更多的精力。我甚至设法去了健身房。

00:32:22.710 --> 00:33:17.690
<v David Rakoff>On day six, I definitely feel something, although not what I expected. I rise early. My energy is high. I feel great. I stand in the bathroom on top of the  spread-out newspaper and give myself my weekly haircut. And then suddenly, my heart begins to race. I am overcome with a shaky weakness and the distinct feeling that I cannot trust my body to do what it is meant to do. Or rather, that what my body is meant to do in that moment is to pass out and have me crack my head against the cold, unforgiving edge of the bath. I get dressed and go to see my doctor a block away. I buy a banana on the way just in case everything goes to hell and I have to end this thing.
<v David Rakoff>在第六天，我确实感觉到了一些东西，尽管不是我预期的那样。我起得很早。我的精力很高。我感觉棒极了。我站在铺开的报纸上面的浴室里，给自己剪了每周一次的头发。然后突然，我的心开始狂跳。我被一种颤抖的虚弱感所征服，并且有一种明确的感觉，即我不能相信我的身体能做它应该做的事情。或者说，我的身体在那一刻注定要做的是晕倒，让我的头撞到冷酷无情的浴缸边缘。我穿上衣服，去看了离这里一个街区远的医生。我在路上买了一根香蕉，以防万一一切都搞砸了，我不得不结束这一切。

00:33:17.690 --> 00:33:36.750
<v David Rakoff>My doctor takes my blood pressure and my pulse, both normal. He's not crazy about the idea of me fasting, but tells me that I am essentially fine, and I return home, my fast intact, the banana uneaten. I call Ira to tell him about it.
<v David Rakoff>医生量了我的血压和脉搏，都很正常。他并不赞成我禁食的想法，但告诉我我基本上没事，我回到了家，我的禁食还在继续，那根香蕉也没吃。我打电话给艾拉告诉他这件事。

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:59.400
<v David Rakoff>Now, I've got this banana, sitting on my desk like a loaded gun. You know, and wasn't it Chekhov who said, you know, if you introduce a gun in the first act, it's got to go off by the third. Here is Ira's suggestion of what to do with this taunting fruit. Maybe it's just the fast, but he seems to be talking double quick.
<v David Rakoff>现在，这根香蕉放在我的桌子上，像一把装满子弹的枪。你知道，契诃夫不是说过吗，如果在第一幕中放了一把枪，它必须在第三幕中发射。这是艾拉对于如何处理这个嘲弄我的水果的建议。也许只是因为禁食，但他说话的速度似乎快了一倍。

00:33:59.400 --> 00:34:00.590
<v Ira Glass>Throw it out the window.
<v Ira Glass>把它扔出窗外。

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:02.470
<v David Rakoff>No, I think what I'm going to do is--
<v David Rakoff>不，我想我要做的是——

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:03.890
<v Ira Glass>Throw it out the window.
<v Ira Glass>把它扔出窗外。

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:09.390
<v David Rakoff>Well, Ira, I live on a street. Throwing it out a window could hit somebody. It's actually against the law probably.
<v David Rakoff>嗯，艾拉，我住在街道上。把它扔出窗外可能会打到人。这实际上可能是违法的。

00:34:09.390 --> 00:34:11.500
<v Ira Glass>You live on the first floor. Throw it out the window.
<v Ira Glass>你住在二楼。把它扔出窗外。

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:26.680
<v David Rakoff>I don't live on the first floor. This is fascinating. Should I be wearing anything special when I throw it out the window?
<v David Rakoff>我不住在二楼。这太迷人了。我把它扔出窗外时应该穿什么特别的衣服吗？

00:34:26.680 --> 00:34:30.940
<v Ira Glass>Does it make you feel safer to have the banana there? Or is the banana taunting you, like seeing--
<v Ira Glass>香蕉放在那儿让你感觉更安全吗？还是香蕉在嘲弄你，就像看到——

00:34:30.940 --> 00:35:08.610
<v David Rakoff>The banana is not really taunting me. There was a point when I thought this morning-- well, I thought a few things and I thought, well, that's it. It's over. And then I thought, ah! Back to the world of food, of interest and diversion. Yahoo! But then I felt like a failure and kind of back to square one, like oh God! Now, how am I going to get through another week of this?
<v David Rakoff>香蕉并没有真正嘲弄我。今天早上确实有一个点——嗯，我想了几件事，我想，嗯，就这样了。结束了。然后我想，啊！回到食物、兴趣和消遣的世界。耶！但随后我觉得自己是个失败者，有点回到原点，就像哦天哪！现在，我该怎么熬过接下来的一个星期？

00:35:08.610 --> 00:36:00.290
<v Ira Glass>Well, it's time for me to make my broth. I have to make this every two days or so. It serves to provide me with electrolytes so that your heart doesn't stop. I was thinking about this lack of enlightenment, but I'm feeling and I sort of thought like I was that woman in the movie Manhattan who was at a party and she says to Woody Allen something like I finally had an orgasm, and my analyst told me it was the wrong kind. I just fell like how come all the other kids are getting enlightened and I'm not?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，是时候做我的肉汤了。我每隔两天左右就要做一次。它用来为我提供电解质，这样你的心脏就不会停止跳动。我当时在想这种启蒙的缺失，但我感觉到了，我有点觉得自己像电影《曼哈顿》里的那个女人，她在派对上对伍迪·艾伦说，她终于有了性高潮，但她的分析师告诉她那是错误的那种。我只是觉得为什么其他孩子都得到了启蒙，而我却没有？

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:07.560
<v Ira Glass>Day nine. Here's something else I'm not feeling, something Ira keeps egging me on about.
<v Ira Glass>第九天。这是另一件我没有感受到的事情，艾拉一直怂恿我的事情。

00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:09.990
<v Ira Glass>Have you started to feel superior?
<v Ira Glass>你开始感到优越了吗？

00:36:09.990 --> 00:36:58.340
<v David Rakoff>Am I walking around, he wants to know, an ethereal creature of light and air, passing by the falafel stand, for example, and looking disdainfully from my slender Olympian perch at the weak-willed humans who feel the need to stuff their gullets with something as earthbound and disgusting as solid nourishment? Not really, as it turns out. This fast hasn't lessened my usual feelings of venality and guilt. If anything, they've increased a little because my days are taken up in this narcissistic rumination about my intake and my output. Between the hours of making the broth and the enemas, this is one of the most self-obsessed things I have ever done in my life. And I say that as a first-person journalist.
<v David Rakoff>他想知道，我是否像一个轻盈空灵的生物一样四处走动，路过沙拉三明治摊，并从我苗条的奥林匹克栖木上，轻蔑地看着那些脆弱的人类，因为他们需要用像固体食物那样世俗而恶心的东西来填饱肚子？事实证明，并非如此。这次禁食并没有减少我通常的卑劣和内疚感。如果说有什么不同的话，那就是它们稍微增加了一些，因为我的日子都花在这种关于我的摄入和排出的自恋沉思中。在做肉汤和灌肠的时间之间，这是我一生中做过的最以自我为中心的事情之一。而且我这是以第一人称记者的身份这么说的。

00:36:58.340 --> 00:37:52.450
<v David Rakoff>One night on the subway, I see a woman at the end of the car. She leans over to the people sitting next to her and asks in a quiet friendly, almost business-like tone, "Do you have any extra food I can buy off of you?" I can only hear her because the train is silent. She isn't standing in the middle of the car addressing us. She's just asking those within earshot. I walk over and give the woman a dollar before I get off at Union Square. "But do you have any extra food?" she asks me. I apologize and say that I do not have any extra food. But I know in that moment that there is neither clarity nor serenity enough in the world that would give me the chutzpah to tell her why not.
<v David Rakoff>一天晚上在地铁上，我看到车厢尽头有一个女人。她俯身向坐在旁边的人询问，用一种安静、友好、几乎像谈生意一样的语气问：“你们有我可以买的额外食物吗？”只有因为火车很安静我才能听到她。她没有站在车厢中间跟我们说话。她只是问了那些在耳闻范围内的人。在我下车前往联合广场之前，我走过去给了那个女人一美元。“但你有什么额外的食物吗？”她问我。我道歉说我没有额外的食物。但我那一刻知道，世界上没有足够的清晰或宁静能给我勇气告诉她为什么没有。

00:37:52.450 --> 00:38:48.690
<v David Rakoff>Day 11. Brian advises everyone on the fast to keep it to themselves and not tell too many people. What I will find, I am told, is that those who know I am fasting will project their own worries or anti-fasting prejudices onto me, saying things like "you look too thin," whereas those who have no idea will simply think I look healthy and great. It seems true. My friends who do know I am fasting think I look gaunt. But then I go to synagogue for the baby-naming service of some friends' twins. I've lost 12 pounds. I wait for the compliments. I find out later that someone asked my friend Jeff, does David have cancer?
<v David Rakoff>第11天。布莱恩建议每个人在禁食期间都要保密，不要告诉太多人。我被告知，我会发现那些知道我在禁食的人会把他们自己的担忧或反禁食偏见投射到我身上，说些“你看起来太瘦了”之类的话，而那些不知道的人只会觉得我看起来健康又棒。这似乎是真的。知道我在禁食的朋友觉得我看起来憔悴。但随后我去犹太教堂参加朋友的双胞胎命名仪式。我瘦了12磅。我等着恭维话。后来我发现有人问我的朋友杰夫，大卫是不是得了癌症？

00:38:48.690 --> 00:39:15.150
<v David Rakoff>Captain's log. I wonder if this is something. I'm curiously relaxed, as if my bones have been removed. How can I explain this? It is as if I have taken an anti-anxiety pill. I am aware of my problems and the things that concern me. Intellectually, they can still freak me out. But my physical anxiety response is gone.
<v David Rakoff>船长日志。不知道这算不算什么。我出奇地放松，就像我的骨头被拆除了一样。我怎么解释呢？就好像我吃了抗焦虑药一样。我知道我的问题和困扰我的事情。理智上，它们仍然可以让我抓狂。但我的身体焦虑反应消失了。

00:39:15.150 --> 00:40:10.750
<v David Rakoff>On the street, a bike messenger zooms by dangerously close to me, nearly shearing off my kneecaps. I can only mutter, "Jerk," where once I might have hissed it. I cannot work myself up into a lather. Do I think, oh, hello, God's creature, fellow and bicycled human. Come ride freely. Let the wind carry you like a seedling, like a bird? No. What I thought was, jerk, I hope you are crushed beneath the wheels of a bus. But the difference was the venom that pulsed through my veins now had the restful back-and-forth rush of the ocean's waves. It's a pleasant feeling, certainly, but hardly seems worth the effort of weeks without food. If this is all there is, then I hate to say it, but Miss Peggy Lee was kind of right.
<v David Rakoff>在街上，一个自行车快递员离我极近地飞驰而过，差点切掉我的膝盖。我只能嘟囔“混蛋”，而在以前我可能会发出嘶嘶声。我无法让自己怒不可遏。我在想，哦，你好，上帝的造物，骑自行车的同类。来吧，自由地骑行吧。让风像幼苗一样，像鸟儿一样带你走吗？不。我当时想的是，混蛋，我希望你被公共汽车轮子碾碎。但不同之处在于，在我血管里跳动的毒液现在有了海洋波浪般平静的起伏感。这确实是一种愉悦的感觉，但似乎不值得为了几周不吃东西而付出努力。如果这就是全部，那么我不想这么说，但佩吉·李小姐说得有点对。

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:53.120
<v David Rakoff>Day 13 without food. It is the home stretch of the fast, and I have lost 14 pounds. I'm not hungry, but I am experiencing appetite. I find myself thinking about food a lot, cooking it almost more than eating it. I pour through books and surf the web for recipes: roast goose with prunes, brown butter madeleines, candied grapefruit peel, precisely the kind of oldy-worldy delicacies Hans Christian Andersen's Little Match Girl saw dancing before her eyes before she froze to death on the Copenhagen street.
<v David Rakoff>第13天没有食物。这是禁食的冲刺阶段，我瘦了14磅。我不饿，但我正在经历食欲。我发现自己经常想食物，做饭的时间几乎比吃东西还多。我翻阅书籍，在网上冲浪寻找食谱：烤鹅配西梅、焦奶油玛德琳、糖渍葡萄柚皮，正是安徒生《卖火柴的小女孩》在哥本哈根街头冻死前，眼中跳舞的那种老式珍馐。

00:40:53.120 --> 00:41:27.510
<v David Rakoff>On the final day of fasting, I bound out of my house, a brilliantly blue and freezing day. I am feeling jaunty and alive, like a character in the movie who, newly in love, walks through the streets he thought he knew, only to find them vibrant and full of beautiful humanity, and he, a wonderful part of it all. He dances for a group of French children. He smiles with friendly commiseration at a young couple, flirts nonthreateningly with an old lady, telling her, "Madam, you smell delicious." He tips his hat to the cop.
<v David Rakoff>禁食的最后一天，我从家里一跃而出，那是一个明亮、寒冷的日子。我感到轻快而充满活力，就像电影里的一个角色，新近陷入爱河，走在他以为熟悉的街道上，却发现它们充满活力，充满美丽的文明，而他，是这一切中美妙的一部分。他为一群法国孩子跳舞。他对着一对年轻夫妇露出友好的同情微笑，与一位老太太进行无威胁的调情，告诉她：“女士，你闻起来真香。”他向警察举了举帽子。

00:41:27.510 --> 00:41:56.930
<v David Rakoff>But it is not universal brotherhood that has me jazzed as I dance along. What I am thinking to myself is, hello world! Tomorrow I eat an apple! My high spirits are tempered with something else, though. A sheepishness that I have somehow failed to receive the thing that I should have, and here I am already out the door, and it's too late.
<v David Rakoff>但我随着舞步跳动时感到的兴奋并非源于普遍的博爱。我内心想的是，你好，世界！明天我吃苹果！然而，我的高昂情绪被别的东西冲淡了。一种羞愧，因为我以某种方式未能获得我应该获得的东西，而现在我已经走出门口，太晚了。

00:41:56.930 --> 00:42:20.090
<v David Rakoff>When I tell Brian that I feel good but not markedly different, he doesn't hesitate to tell me that my experience is atypical. From Brian's perspective, I didn't approach this with an open mind. I need to talk to someone who's managed to achieve what I did not. I compare notes with Amy Warren, who has fasted five times.
<v David Rakoff>当我告诉布莱恩我感觉很好但没有明显不同时，他毫不犹豫地告诉我，我的经历是不典型的。从布莱恩的角度来看，我没有以开放的心态接近这件事。我需要和那些成功做到我没做到的人谈谈。我比较了艾米·沃伦的笔记，她已经禁食了五次。

00:42:20.090 --> 00:42:34.870
<v Amy Warren>When I read the pamphlet, initially I was like, oh, this is all about cleansing and getting rid of toxins that have been building up in your system. And I found that first time I did the fast, that was the most satisfying in terms of feeling that the toxins were leaving my body.
<v Amy Warren>当我读到小册子时，起初我觉得，哦，这全是关于清洁和摆脱在你系统中积聚的毒素。我发现我第一次禁食时，就感到毒素正在离开我的身体，这在那种感觉上是最令人满足的。

00:42:34.870 --> 00:42:36.490
<v David Rakoff>And you felt that that happened?
<v David Rakoff>你觉得那真的发生了？

00:42:36.490 --> 00:42:38.950
<v Amy Warren>Yes, I saw it. I mean, I saw it.
<v Amy Warren>是的，我看到了。我的意思是，我看到了。

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:40.680
<v David Rakoff>What form did it take?
<v David Rakoff>它采取了什么形式？

00:42:40.680 --> 00:42:43.470
<v Amy Warren>Well, is it appropriate to talk about on the radio?
<v Amy Warren>嗯，在广播里谈论这个合适吗？

00:42:43.470 --> 00:42:46.170
<v David Rakoff>Oh, OK, so it took that form. I see.
<v David Rakoff>哦，好的，所以它采取了那种形式。我明白了。

00:42:46.170 --> 00:42:58.970
<v Amy Warren>I mean, it might sound really insane and gross. But the stuff that would come out of you, you would be, like, where-- what is that? You wouldn't even know what it was. It looked like from a movie, like, it looked like unreal.
<v Amy Warren>我的意思是，这听起来可能真的很疯狂和恶心。但从你身体里出来的东西，你会想，哪里——那是什么？你甚至不知道那是什么。看起来像电影里的，看起来不真实。

00:42:58.970 --> 00:43:01.660
<v David Rakoff>I was just going to say, by a movie, you don't mean Room with a View?
<v David Rakoff>我正想说，说电影，你不是指《看得见风景的房间》吧？

00:43:01.660 --> 00:43:36.030
<v Amy Warren>No. I mean a movie like in Star Wars where in that sewer and the water and all that sludge and slime and weird bubbly things, and just misshapened, odd-- what is that? It's all there, and it comes out of you. And then you're like, what? And when everything's running clear, like water from a faucet, you feel like you're new, like you're a new person. And you feel like you're sexy and like alive. And you feel like you're somehow your highest self. There's nothing you couldn't do.
<v Amy Warren>不。我的意思是像《星球大战》那样的电影，在那个下水道里，水和所有的污泥、粘液和奇怪的泡沫东西，还有形状扭曲、奇怪的——那是什么？它就在那里，从你身体里出来。然后你会想，什么？当一切都变得清澈，就像水龙头里的水一样，你觉得你是新的，就像你是换了一个人。你觉得你很性感，很有生命力。你觉得自己以某种方式成了最高的自我。没有什么你做不到的。

00:43:36.030 --> 00:43:36.810
<v David Rakoff>Really?
<v David Rakoff>真的吗？

00:43:36.810 --> 00:43:49.630
<v Amy Warren>In terms of your life and your goals and everything, you feel sort of focused and clarified and like you know what you want to do, and you feel like you could do anything. You feel empowered.
<v Amy Warren>在你的生活、目标和一切方面，你觉得你有点专注和清晰，你觉得你知道你想做什么，而且你觉得你可以做任何事情。你觉得自己被赋予了权力。

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:55.390
<v David Rakoff>Really? Wow! I did it wrong.
<v David Rakoff>真的吗？哇！我做错了。

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:58.120
<v Amy Warren>Oh, no, you didn't do it wrong at all.
<v Amy Warren>哦，不，你一点也没做错。

00:43:58.120 --> 00:44:36.650
<v David Rakoff>I think I did, but it's OK. But I didn't feel that clarity. I mean, I feel good. But I feel just as good when I've had a tiny bit of caffeine and, you know, that it's exactly it, and I've exercised or something. So I don't really know. I feel like everybody's like, oh, where were you? Paris. Oh, did you see the Eiffel Tower? Or the Louvre? The Louvre, I couldn't see that. That somehow, I managed to miss everything. They would say like, oh, well, how was the food? Was it wonderful? Well, there was a vending machine and some Junior Mints.
<v David Rakoff>我觉得我做了，但没关系。但我没感觉到那种清晰。我的意思是，我感觉很好。但我喝了一点咖啡因，你知道，就是那样，我锻炼了一下，我也会感觉很好。所以我真的不知道。我觉得大家就像，哦，你去哪儿了？巴黎。哦，你看到埃菲尔铁塔了吗？还是卢浮宫？卢浮宫，我没看到。我以某种方式错过了所有东西。他们会说，哦，好吧，食物怎么样？美妙吗？嗯，有一台自动售货机和一些薄荷糖。

00:44:36.650 --> 00:45:15.270
<v Ira Glass>In a little unscientific survey conducted by the producers of this show, calling around to friends, and friends of friends who'd fasted, and even four people who specifically did my fast, albeit for much longer, they found that everyone felt great physically, energetic and alert, and nearly everybody spoke about a clarity of mind. But only one person seemed to feel more than that. This woman said that for a moment while driving, her spirit was filled up and she was overcome with a sense of total well-being, of a level and profundity she'd never felt before. Amy's experience was more typical.
<v Ira Glass>在节目制作人进行的一次小规模非科学调查中，他们给禁食的朋友和朋友的朋友打了电话，甚至包括四位专门进行我的禁食法的长久禁食者，他们发现每个人身体上都感觉很好，充满活力和警觉，几乎每个人都谈到了头脑的清晰。但只有一个人似乎感受到了更多。这个女人说，在开车时有一瞬间，她的精神被填满了，她被一种前所未有的全面幸福感所征服。艾米的经历更典型。

00:45:15.270 --> 00:45:33.590
<v Amy Warren>When I first started doing it, I was thinking like, yeah, I'm going to-- it's going to be like spiritual nirvana. I'm going to recognize the universe in a way that I've never recognized the universe. But I really feel like that expectation, you know, it was-- it never happened.
<v Amy Warren>当我刚开始做的时候，我在想，是的，我会——它会像精神上的涅槃。我会以一种我从未认识过的方式认识宇宙。但我真的觉得那种期望，你知道，它是——它从来没有发生过。

00:45:33.590 --> 00:45:35.080
<v David Rakoff>It never happened?
<v David Rakoff>它从来没有发生过？

00:45:35.080 --> 00:45:57.030
<v Amy Warren>No. Not in a way that I would have sort of expected it to. I mean, I did definitely-- I always felt a sense of clarity and focus, different than any other time in my life, but I never felt like it was a religious or spiritual thing.
<v Amy Warren>不。不是我预期的那种方式。我的意思是，我确实总是感觉到一种清晰和专注，与我生命中任何时候都不同，但我从没觉得它是一种宗教或精神上的东西。

00:45:57.030 --> 00:46:23.000
<v David Rakoff>It's Wednesday morning at about 7:30, and today is the apple. On the first day I'm allowed to eat, before I have my apple, I'm supposed to mix a tablespoon of bran and flaxseed into the broth. It makes it as thick as porridge, and it knocks me out.
<v David Rakoff>现在是周三早上7:30左右，今天是苹果日。在被允许吃东西的第一天，吃苹果之前，我应该在一勺麸皮和亚麻籽中混合肉汤。它让它变得像粥一样浓稠，这让我很受挫。

00:46:23.000 --> 00:47:12.610
<v David Rakoff>OK, it's an hour and a half later. I hadn't thought that eating would be quite this difficult. And I suppose I'd better try and eat this apple. It's very strange. I looked forward to it for so long, and now it just feels like a little bit of an ordeal. All right. [BITES AN APPLE AND CHEWS] Here I am, chewing fruit again. I almost feel like I was never away from it.
<v David Rakoff>好的，一个半小时后了。我没想到吃东西会这么困难。我想我最好试着吃这个苹果。这很奇怪。我期待它这么久，现在它感觉像是一种折磨。好吧。[咬一口苹果并咀嚼]我又在这儿吃水果了。我几乎觉得我从未离开过它。

00:47:12.610 --> 00:48:00.550
<v David Rakoff>Right when I started the fast, I had the briefest romantic notion that it wouldn't just work, but it really would be the magic bullet. I entertained the fantasy that the fast would short-circuit logic, and somehow, my problems, such as they are, didn't need the talk and the scrutiny. They needed this. There was even a moment where I could see my problems, feel them, impacted and concentrated into a grey, plaque-like obstruction. A thick, squat blockage the shape and size of a scallop somewhere in the channels of my body. And I thought that this was the way that they would be pulverized and broken up and flushed away.
<v David Rakoff>当我刚开始禁食时，我有一个极其短暂的浪漫念头，认为它不仅会奏效，而且它真的会是魔法子弹。我幻想着禁食会绕过逻辑，以某种方式，我的问题，比如它们是什么，不需要谈话和审查。它们需要这个。甚至有那么一刻，我能看到我的问题，感受到它们，像灰色的、斑块状的阻塞一样冲击和集中。一个厚实、低矮、大小形状像扇贝的堵塞物在我身体的通道里。我以为这就是它们被粉碎、打散并冲走的方式。

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:45.420
<v David Rakoff>Now that I've finished the fast and am on the other side, it's hard to remember exactly what I was waiting for, although I do know that it was something wholly unfamiliar and thrilling, like a new color, one I'd never seen before. Not a mixture, no trace of blue or yellow or red. What would that look like? Even though our physical world makes the existence of such a thing basically impossible, I'd still really like to see that.
<v David Rakoff>既然我已经完成了禁食，身处另一边，很难准确记得我在等待什么，虽然我知道那是一种完全陌生和令人兴奋的东西，就像一种新颜色，我以前从未见过的那种。不是混合物，没有蓝色、黄色或红色的痕迹。那看起来会是什么样子？尽管我们的物理世界使得这种东西的存在基本上是不可能的，但我仍然真的很想看看。

00:48:45.420 --> 00:48:49.810
<v Ira Glass>David Rakoff. He's put the story of his fast into his brand-new book Don't Get Too Comfortable.
<v Ira Glass>大卫·拉科夫。他把禁食的故事写进了他的新书《别太舒服》。

00:48:49.810 --> 00:49:49.910
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "EAT IT" BY WEIRD AL YANKOVIC]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 韦尔·奥尔·扬科维奇的《吃掉它》]

00:49:49.910 --> 00:50:02.640
<v Ira Glass>Act Three. Mystery Train. We close our show today with the story of two people, each earning for certain future, though not necessarily the same future. Hillary Frank remembers the story.
<v Ira Glass>第三章。神秘列车。今天我们以两个人的故事结束节目，每个人都渴望某种未来，尽管不一定是同一个未来。希拉里·弗兰克记得这个故事。

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:43.280
<v Hillary Frank>The lights were out. We were stopped on the tracks, and Danielle wasn't happy. "All I wanted," she said, "was to have McDonald's with my boyfriend." It was supposed to be their big Friday night in Boston. They were sitting diagonally behind me. Danielle and her boyfriend Donny had gotten on the train in Providence a few stops after mine. Eavesdropping in the dark, I learned their names, that they were 15-- four years younger than me-- and that they were surprised to see the girl sitting by the window behind me. Donny's surprise was pleasant. "Man, Cynthia, I didn't expect to run into you," and Danielle's was more like, "yeah, didn't think I'd run into you."
<v Hillary Frank>灯熄灭了。我们停在铁轨上，丹妮尔很不高兴。“我想要的，”她说，“只是和我的男朋友去吃麦当劳。”这本该是他们在波士顿度过的一个盛大的周五晚上。他们坐在我斜后方。丹妮尔和她的男朋友多尼在Providence我下车的几站后上了火车。在黑暗中偷听，我得知了他们的名字，他们才15岁——比我小四岁——而且他们很惊讶看到那个坐在我后面靠窗的女孩。多尼的惊讶很愉快。“天哪，辛西娅，我没想到会遇见你，”而丹妮尔的惊讶更像是，“是啊，没想到会遇见你。”

00:50:43.280 --> 00:51:29.210
<v Hillary Frank>Other than an elderly couple in the back, we were the only people in the car. Cynthia told them it was her 18th birthday. She invited Danielle and Donny to the party her sister was throwing for her in Boston. "I'll get you good and wasted," she told them. Donny practically cheered. Danielle must have punched him because he winced out loud. "I ain't doin' none of that," she said. "Yeah, you are," Donny teased. "You're gettin' doped." "Am not," she said. "You know I can't do that. None of it. You know." "Come on," Cynthia prodded. "It'll make your man happy." "No," Danielle repeated. She sounded like she was going to cry. "I can't. I can't."
<v Hillary Frank>除了后面的一对老年夫妇，车厢里只有我们。辛西娅告诉他们这是她18岁生日。她邀请丹妮尔和多尼去她姐姐在波士顿为她举办的派对。“我会让你喝得烂醉，”她告诉他们。多尼差点欢呼起来。丹妮尔一定打了他的拳头，因为他大声叫痛。“我才不要做那种事，”她说。“你会的，”多尼取笑说。“你会嗨起来的。”“才不会，”她说。“你知道我不能那样做。根本不行。你知道。”“来吧，”辛西娅催促道。“这会让你的男人高兴的。”“不，”丹妮尔重复道。她听起来快要哭了。“我不能。我不能。”

00:51:29.210 --> 00:52:07.330
<v Hillary Frank>The lights came back on. The engine revved, and we inched along the tracks. Everyone sat quietly for a minute. Cynthia spoke first. "How long you been together?" "Five months," Danielle answered. Cynthia seemed to be working up to something. "How long have you known?" "Three months," Danielle said quietly. Cynthia's seat creaked. "I was once, too," she told Danielle. "A couple years ago." Pregnant, I thought.
<v Hillary Frank>灯亮了。引擎轰鸣，我们沿着轨道慢慢爬行。每个人都静静地坐了一分钟。辛西娅先开口。“你们在一起多久了？”“五个月，”丹妮尔回答。辛西娅似乎在酝酿什么。“你知道多久了？”“三个月，”丹妮尔低声说。辛西娅的座位吱呀作响。“我曾经也是，”她告诉丹妮尔。“几年前。”怀孕了，我想。

00:52:07.330 --> 00:52:22.680
<v Hillary Frank>We were back up to speed. The conductor poked his head into our car to tell us we'd been delayed because some juveniles had placed shopping carts on the tracks. It seemed strange for him to be using the word juveniles when we were all teenagers ourselves.
<v Hillary Frank>我们又回到了速度。列车长把头探进车厢，告诉我们延误是因为一些青少年把购物推车放在了轨道上。当我们在座的都是青少年时，他使用“青少年”这个词显得很奇怪。

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:55.620
<v Hillary Frank>Cynthia asked Donny if he loved Danielle. "Of course I love her," he said. "She's mine." "If you really did, then you wouldn't have kissed Angel," Danielle snapped. "Angel?" Cynthia asked. She scooted over to the aisle seat. "Yeah," Danielle said. "He kissed her two weeks ago, even when he knew she had AIDS." "Not a real kiss," Donny said. "What do you mean not a real kiss?" Cynthia asked. "It was nothing," he told her. "Yeah right. Nothing," Danielle shot back.
<v Hillary Frank>辛西娅问多尼是否爱丹妮尔。“我当然爱她，”他说。“她是我的。”“如果你真的爱她，那你就不会亲安吉尔，”丹妮尔厉声说。“安吉尔？”辛西娅问。她滑向过道边的座位。“是啊，”丹妮尔说。“他两周前亲了她，即使他知道她有艾滋病。”“不是真正的亲吻，”多尼说。“什么叫不是真正的亲吻？”辛西娅问。“什么都不是，”他告诉她。“是啊，对。什么都不是，”丹妮尔反驳道。

00:52:55.620 --> 00:53:36.330
<v Hillary Frank>Cynthia leaned over the aisle towards Donny. "Show me," she said. "Show you what?" Cynthia slid back to the window seat and patted the cushion beside her. "Show me how you kissed Angel," she told him. As Donnie crossed the aisle, Danielle yelled at him. "Don't show her! Tell her!" "It's OK," Cynthia said calmly. "I mean no disrespect. Say it with me, no disrespect." "Fine," Danielle mumbled through clenched teeth. "No disrespect." "So show me," Cynthia said.
<v Hillary Frank>辛西娅越过过道向多尼倾斜。“给我看看，”她说。“看什么？”辛西娅滑回靠窗的座位，拍了拍旁边的垫子。“给我看看你是怎么亲安吉尔的，”她告诉他。当多尼穿过过道时，丹妮尔对他大喊。“别给她看！告诉她！”“没关系，”辛西娅平静地说。“我没有不尊重。跟我一起说，没有不尊重。”“好吧，”丹妮尔咬牙切齿地咕哝着。“没有不尊重。”“所以给我看看，”辛西娅说。

00:53:36.330 --> 00:53:52.190
<v Hillary Frank>There was a soft smooching sound, which must have been a peck on the cheek, because both of the girls shouted "No way!" in unison. "I know that's not how he did it," Danielle said. Cynthia laughed. "Show me for real," she commanded.
<v Hillary Frank>传来了轻柔的亲吻声，那一定是脸颊上的轻吻，因为两个女孩异口同声地喊道：“没门！”“我知道他不是那样做的，”丹妮尔说。辛西娅笑了。“来真的给我看看，”她命令道。

00:53:52.190 --> 00:54:13.630
<v Hillary Frank>Tonguey mouth smacks were followed by Danielle tearing at Donny's back. There was slapping and whining verging on tears, and Cynthia's voice saying, "Chill, girl. I stuck my tongue in his mouth, not the other way around," as if that made it OK. Then she added, "You kissed Angel like that? You can't love this one that much."
<v Hillary Frank>带舌头的嘴巴撞击声之后是丹妮尔抓扯多尼的背部。有拍打和近乎哭泣的抱怨，还有辛西娅的声音说：“冷静点，女孩。我把舌头伸进了他的嘴里，而不是反过来，”好像那让这变得可以接受了。然后她补充道：“你就是这样亲安吉尔的？你不可能有多爱这个。”

00:54:13.630 --> 00:54:37.520
<v Hillary Frank>It went on like this for around 20 minutes. Cynthia asking Donny if he'd ever cheat on Danielle. Donny saying no. Cynthia saying, "You wouldn't? Even if I did this to you?" Donnie buckling to temptation, and Danielle grumbling in her corner with occasional outbursts of fury.
<v Hillary Frank>这种情况持续了大约20分钟。辛西娅问多尼是否会对丹妮尔不忠。多尼说不会。辛西娅说：“你不会吗？即使我对你做这个？”多尼屈服于诱惑，丹妮尔在角落里咕哝着，偶尔爆发出一阵愤怒。

00:54:37.520 --> 00:55:12.900
<v Hillary Frank>Finally, Cynthia stopped her come-ons and said, "You are sick. You would do all that with me and you say you love her?" "But I do," Donny said uncertainly. "I'm going to be with her 'til the day I die." "You should dump him," Cynthia told Danielle. Then she asked Donny if he had a place to stay. Danielle answered for him. "25 Maple Street with me and my mom." "Well, if that ever changes," Cynthia said, "you'll always have a place in Boston at my sister's apartment. You can see it right now if you come to the party."
<v Hillary Frank>最后，辛西娅停止了挑逗，说：“你有病。你会和我做这一切，然后你说你爱她？”“但我确实爱，”多尼不确定地说。“我会和她在一起直到我死的那天。”“你应该甩了他，”辛西娅告诉丹妮尔。然后她问多尼有没有地方住。丹妮尔替他回答了。“枫树街25号，我和我妈那里。”“好吧，如果情况有变，”辛西娅说，“你在波士顿我姐姐公寓里总有一席之地。如果你来参加派对，你现在就可以看到它。”

00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:40.010
<v Hillary Frank>The train slowed as we entered Back Bay station. "We're going," Donny announced. "I'm going to McDonald's," Danielle said. "Girl, come on. It'll be fun," Cynthia said. "She's going," Donny said. "She's going and gettin' so doped." "Am not." They were still going back and forth like this by the time we got off the train. I stood there watching them on the platform, wondering what Danielle would end up doing.
<v Hillary Frank>火车驶入后湾站时减速了。“我们要走了，”多尼宣布。“我要去吃麦当劳，”丹妮尔说。“女孩，来吧。会很有趣的，”辛西娅说。“她要去，”多尼说。“她要去嗨起来。”“才不会。”当我们需要下火车时，他们还在争论。我站在站台上看着他们，好奇丹妮尔最终会做什么。

00:55:40.010 --> 00:56:18.560
<v Hillary Frank>I still think of her sometimes. I end the evening in my head. On bad days, Danielle goes to the party. She watches Donny drink spiked punch and smoke pot and maybe settle into a corner with a cute girl in a halter top. Sometimes she winds up at McDonald's by herself, washing down a big Mac and supersized fries with a chocolate milkshake. But on good days, Danielle crosses the tracks, sits quietly on a bench, and waits for the next train back to Providence.
<v Hillary Frank>我偶尔还会想起她。我在脑海里结束了这个夜晚。在糟糕的日子里，丹妮尔去了派对。她看着多尼喝着加了料的酒，抽着大麻，也许和一个穿挂脖衫的漂亮女孩在角落里安定下来。有时她独自一人去了麦当劳，用巧克力奶昔冲下一份巨无霸和超大号薯条。但在美好的日子里，丹妮尔穿过铁轨，安静地坐在长椅上，等待下一班回Providence的火车。

00:56:18.560 --> 00:56:23.930
<v Ira Glass>Hillary Frank is the author of the novels Better Than Running at Night and I Can't Tell You.
